course: dictionary
lesson: 4039
title: One word, three meanings: gee
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Gee is a good example. Here it is a short word that shows a feeling: «Gee, thanks!» The word shows surprise and thanks, so it is an exclamation, not the name of anything.
explain: Gee also appears in the phrase gee up. Riders say it to a horse to make it go faster, and in the UK people say to gee someone up when they mean to encourage them. Gee is also the name of the letter G. The words around it are your clues: if the sentence is about a horse, you know which meaning is right.
explain: Gee began as a way of avoiding bad language. Long ago people thought it was wrong to say God's or Jesus's name in anger, so they changed the words. «Gee» took the place of Jesus. Today it is a mild word, mostly used in America, and it sounds much gentler than the name it came from.
fact: Gee is an interjection when it shows surprise, delight or mild annoyance, like «Gee whiz!»
fact: "Gee up" is what riders say to a horse to make it go faster.
fact: In the UK, to gee someone up means to encourage them.
fact: Gee is the name of the letter G.
fact: «Gee» is a mild word used instead of "Jesus", and it is mostly American. Gosh, golly and jeepers are similar mild stand-ins.
exercise: «Gee, thanks for the present!» What does gee show here? | A sleepy yawn | *Surprise and thanks | The name of a letter | A horse going faster
exercise: The rider shouted «gee up» to the horse. What did the rider want? | The horse to lie down and sleep | To hear the word spelt out | The horse to tell a joke | *The horse to go faster
exercise: Which word is a mild stand-in for "Jesus"? | *Gee | Golly, the sea | Ouch | Hooray
test: «Gee, I did not expect to see you here!» What is gee showing? | A sum of money | A sleepy yawn | *Surprise | A word for a horse's bridle
test: In the UK, what does it mean to "gee someone up"? | To put them to sleep | To count them one by one | To tell them off | *To encourage them
exam: Which sentence uses gee as the name of a letter? | "Gee whiz, you won!" | "Gee up, horse!" | *"Gee is the seventh letter of the alphabet." | "Gee, thanks a lot."
